WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.680
Series Inside the Cell. Season 1 episode 1

00:12.480 --> 00:16.200
Um, why do I have to learn about boring old cells anyway?

00:16.200 --> 00:34.920
Hi there, I'm Selly, your friendly neighborhood cell. Um, well, I guess I'm more like your friendly inside the body cell.

00:34.920 --> 00:36.760
Huh? Who said that?

00:36.760 --> 00:40.160
Down here. Wanna see what I mean? Make a wish.

00:40.160 --> 00:43.160
Uh, I wish I could understand cells better.

00:43.160 --> 00:44.280
Ta-da!

00:44.280 --> 00:59.120
Wow, everything's so big. Or am I small?

00:59.120 --> 01:01.680
You're cell size now, ready for an adventure.

01:01.680 --> 01:02.520
You bet.

01:14.280 --> 01:32.200
Welcome to the world of the cell, Miko. Let me show you around. See that big blob in the middle? That's the nucleus, the cell's control center.

01:32.200 --> 01:34.960
It looks like a giant glow-in-the-dark bean.

01:34.960 --> 01:40.040
And those squiggly things floating around? They're mitochondria, the powerhouses of the cell.

01:40.040 --> 01:44.200
They look like little batteries.

01:45.200 --> 01:54.000
Great observation. Now see those flat pancake-like things? That's the endoplasmic reticulum. It's like the cell's highway system.

01:54.000 --> 01:55.800
Whoa, there's so much going on.

01:55.800 --> 01:59.160
And we haven't even gotten to the best part yet. Look over there.

01:59.160 --> 02:01.600
It looks like a bunch of bubbles stuck together.

02:01.600 --> 02:12.200
That's the Golgi apparatus. It's like the cell's post office, packaging and sending materials where they need to go.

02:12.320 --> 02:15.400
This is amazing, but what's that alarm sound?

02:15.400 --> 02:20.720
Oh no, we're low on energy. Quick, Miko. We need to help the mitochondria make more ATP.

02:20.720 --> 02:22.640
ATP? What's that?

02:22.640 --> 02:41.720
It's the energy currency of the cell. Come on, I'll show you how we make it.

02:41.720 --> 02:46.400
See those glucose molecules? Push them into the mitochondria.

02:46.400 --> 02:48.440
Like this? Ha!

02:48.440 --> 02:55.080
Perfect. Now watch as the mitochondria break them down and, presto, ATP energy packets.

02:55.080 --> 02:57.240
We did it. The alarm stopped.

02:57.240 --> 03:02.560
Great job, Miko. You've just helped power the cell. Ta-da, we just made a new cell.

03:02.560 --> 03:06.440
I can't wait to tell everyone about my adventure inside the cell.

03:06.440 --> 03:10.920
Remember, Miko, every cell in your body is working hard to keep you healthy and happy.

03:10.920 --> 03:14.040
Thanks, Selly. I'll never look at biology the same way again.

